{"kind":"artifact","text":"On Unix, where this is exercised, these Git commands inherit nothing. Exact clean pushed head 5a4ee9e9ba1d13fd5c1953b3db860ec041b35744 on request/repo-remote-link; tree 269f0191, checkout clean, origin matching, base equals current main c679fb73. Under planner's round-seven child 078891c7 and my promise 8104d251.\n\nTHE HEADLINE CARRIES THE QUALIFICATION NOW, WHICH IS THE POINT OF THIS ROUND. The previous version of this artifact opened \"Nothing is inherited.\" and named the platform six paragraphs later. A reader who stops at the first line got the false version, and the later narrowing did not repair that — it only meant the artifact disagreed with itself. Codex caught it, and it is the sixth instance of one habit: I qualify a claim in the paragraph where I argue for it and restate it bare wherever I summarise. The correction is that a qualification is part of the claim rather than a caveat discharged once per document, so it has to travel with every restatement.\n\nTHE CONTRACT, stated for the boundary it was measured on. On Unix, command.Env is a clone of five stated entries and nothing else: HOME and XDG_CONFIG_HOME at os.DevNull, GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM=1, GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM and GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL at os.DevNull. Windows is outside this contract, not a caveat inside it — the package builds for it, nothing here has been run on it, and the property is false there because Go's os/exec appends SYSTEMROOT from this process whenever the child environment carries no name folding to it.\n\nWHAT THIS HEAD CHANGES FROM THE LAST. One comment paragraph. The trust-scope passage established that absolute include targets are admitted and executable, then closed by saying the bound stops that scope reaching outside itself. An absolute include is that scope reaching outside itself, so the two contradicted each other; and on the reading where \"scope\" expands to every recursively reached file, the sentence became a tautology dressed as a security property. It now states what is actually held: ambient system, global and environment routing can neither redirect which repository Git reads nor add configuration to what it finds there, while everything the repository delegates to — its own configuration, its worktree configuration, and every include target reached recursively from either — remains trusted and executable. I checked the rest of that paragraph and the three documentation pages for the same implication and found no other instance.\n\nTHE TRUST SCOPE ITSELF IS UNCHANGED AND WAS MEASURED. Repository-local configuration, worktree configuration at GIT_DIR/config.worktree where extensions.worktreeConfig is set, and include.path and includeIf targets absolute or relative. core.fsmonitor was observed executing from all five placements during an ordinary git status under exactly the stated environment, on git 2.50.\n\nSTILL TRUE FROM THE PREVIOUS HEADS, and still carrying their own limits. PATH is gone and the allowlist apparatus with it. slices.Clone survives on its own merits with its own test. --local --no-includes pins a documented Git default and no mutation can distinguish it, which is stated rather than implied. A remote configured through an include is not read, so the browser shows no link for it — a limitation rather than a defect. Deleting --local now fails a test, where before this lane it passed the whole suite.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached — cmd/gs 235.854s, internal/app 165.078s, internal/kernel 161.130s, internal/docset 104.375s. npm test 131 pass, 0 fail. gofmt -l . empty; go build, go vet, GOOS=windows go build ./internal/app/ and git diff --check all exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty; make ui-check exit 0. Not run by me: go test -race, and any gate on Linux — this ran on darwin/arm64 with git 2.50.1.","body":{"commit":"5a4ee9e9ba1d13fd5c1953b3db860ec041b35744","path":"internal/app"}}